00:00:00.760 --> 00:00:06.309 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  hello and welcome to the  values of the west debate now the west  one saw itself as the driver of global  progress  we were confident that enlightenment  values would guarantee success  uncover knowledge and create progressive  social change  but now many of us are beset with doubt  critical of our past we have little  faith in the future and  in the power of our core beliefs to  bring about a better world  so is this self-criticism a welcome and  less arrogant  outlook a consequence perhaps of western  imperial guilt and  relative economic decline or should we  overcome our doubts and reignite faith  in our values and  in their power to improve lives or  should we instead just escape our  history  to fashion a new set of ideals that  we're proud to endorse  and which drives success all very  difficult questions but luckily we've  got a great panel  to help us disentangle them so kai indi  andrews  is an academic activist and author who  appears frequently on  tv radio podcast online to discuss his  research  in black studies miriam francois is a  broadcaster and journalist who's a  research associate at the school of  oriental and african studies  and her website is we need to talk about  whiteness.com  and physician philosopher and novelist  raymond tallis  is emeritus professor of geriatric  medicine at the university of manchester  and has been described as one of the  world's greatest living polymaths  so lucky us now what i'm going to do is  to ask each other three of them  to do their three-minute pitch on the  question of  why have we come to have doubts about  the values that made the west successful  and i'm going to start with kai indy  good afternoon so  i mean i think the first thing to start  with is to say that the myths that we've  been given about our society is  essentially a lie  uh we've been told that the  enlightenment these great values  um built the west that was built on  industrial progress and it's this great  march forward into history  and that has always been untrue and  what's what's happened there  is you have children from the former  colonies here i like myself  have platforms have jobs in academia and  are able to be a bit more critical and  to say well actually  the kind of progress narrative of the  industrial revolution political  revolution  and scientific revolution is partly true  but actually the west was also built on  slavery colonialism  and genocide and actually when we look  at what the real values of the west are  we will therefore find that racism  patriarchy  violence these are all deeply embedded  in what the western project  has always been and now you have people  with a platform to say that that's  that's what  that's what's caused this existential  crisis i mean i work in universities  up until relatively recently the  university was the place the province  the safe space  uh for rich white men in 1960 only five  percent of the population went to  universities was elite  white men so we shouldn't be surprised  that we've had this very narrow  uh progress narrative and now that's  opened up and you have some more  critical voices we ask time to look at  and say well actually how is the world  really  shaped you couldn't have had the  industrial revolution without slavery  and colonialism  and genocide you couldn't have had the  foundation of western science and  modernity without  uh it's the kind of colonial violence  that's taken claim to millions upon  millions of lives  there is no political or an economic  system that has been more violent and  there's  more death to the world than western  liberalism uh  just from from its very inception and  also  we think about these things as being  historical things have passed and we've  progressed into this newer age  but one statistic alone would debunk  that whole thing a child dies every 10  seconds because they don't have access  to food they know and almost all of  those children are black and brown  children in the underdeveloped world  and kovit19 has made us have a kind of  existential look at this there's so many  people dying in the west  nine million people die from poverty  every single  year and all those nine million people  who live our black and brown lives  almost all of them have black and brown  lives  in the developing world and what kovid  has shown us is actually  he just brought in the experience that  this is how most people live where  actually  this is a matter of life and death so  when we think about the values of the  west we have to be thinking  about a proper reappraisal racism  patriarchy  violence those are really the values of  the west that sustain it as much today  as sustained in the past  and we need to have an honest  conversation about what that means  okay thank you miriam  so why have we come to have doubts about  the values that made the west successful  um i think there's a lot for me  personally to unpick in that question  the first  one is whenever we use the term we i  would question  who it is that gets to speak as we um  because certainly the idea that these  doubts are new  for everyone who is identified as a  citizen  of this very fluid notion of the west  um is is an erroneous one i think not  everyone's experiences  of the mythology of the greatness of  western values  um is suddenly being uh placed into  question now i would  um obviously be referring if we want to  talk about  more recent history people like uh  gretel gokan who the 80  one year nhs nurse um who was deported  back to jamaica from the um  the a woman who came from the windrush  generation uh brit  britain british served britain in the  nhs  and then obviously her identification  with  the west being placed into question um  i'm not sure that's a new  uh experience for uh people like gretel  i would also  argue that it's probably uh the  perception or the mythology of western  greatness  as is normalized within centers of  empire is probably not shared  by the relatives of the 17 million  indians who died of hunger  between 1891 and 1900s so i mean  this idea that there is even a consensus  over  what constitutes western values and  their quote-unquote successes is  to be placed into question quite  seriously i would argue  a second point is about the this notion  of the west  um it's a term that becomes popularized  i would argue during the cold war era  when we have this idea of the world  being divided between two blocks  but it's also worth thinking quite  profoundly about what it is  that this term means it's obviously a  social construct we're not talking about  a territorially defined entity it's  usually meant to include  america and the uk does it include  europe if it includes europe do we  include hungary which is currently led  by a fascist  do we include the fascists within  america  and the uk do we include israel which is  placed in the  center of the middle east um and so in  that sense  i think the notion of the west itself is  is a  what i call a power play which is an  assertion  of a unified uh construct  when in fact it's not very unified and  it  essentially serves to delineate who's  excluded from  that camp and my final point would be  about the west's  success can we just be very careful  about what we mean by success do we mean  success in terms of um you know  capitalism  the fact that a large amount of wealth  has been accrued by  a small minority of people primarily on  the backs of people  in the so-called developing world um but  it also off the back of many  people who are descendants of those who  were exploited and who live  today in the uk in varying levels of  poverty and so  i'm not sure who gets to define that  success but currently i think it does  require  perhaps a bit more of a nuanced  assessment than the current mythology  would have us believe  okay thank you and ray talis i have to  say i share  most of the concerns that have been  expressed by miriam and cayender  certainly it's obvious that the material  success and political power of the west  was to an important degree based on  empire building  and contrary to the story i was told at  school the colonisers were not engaged  in spreading truth enlightenment  civilization and prosperity  across an ignorant and unenlightened  world we are now coming to see  too little too late perhaps the extent  to which colonialism was built on racism  the presumption of white supremacy and  involved genocide enslavement and  exploitation  and there is a powerful argument that  the continuing global inequities  are to some extent the continuation of  the colonial era by other means  however to what extent was what orwell  described as  excuse me again as the dirty work of  empire the product of western values  distinctly or uniquely western values  the barbarism of the colonial era was  not unique slavery has been and remains  a human universal  the slave trade in classical greece and  rome and the arabic slave trade  are a reminder of this servitude and  destitution  justified by the high-minded often those  called by their gods to enslave others  has been a feature of all societies and  other societies not particularly driven  by western values  have embedded servitude in for example  caste systems  and it continues worldwide today in the  appalling discrepancy between those who  have the power of consumption  and those who toil to produce so does it  follow that we should reject  the values that made the west successful  some values we should most certainly  reject  indeed they can hardly be called values  the assumption of a god-given right to  invade and possess  lands belonging to other people  propelled by a myth of spreading truth  and progress across the world  but there are other questions how much  of the barbarism empire was value driven  or was it a matter of something more  universal greed  arrogance the misuse of power and  secondly are there other professed  values that it would be a  disaster to jettison among the  enlightenment  professed commitment to universal rights  to justice  to democracy and to an approach to  understanding the world that is based in  science would we wish to get us on that  to continue watching this video click  the link in the top left or in the  description below  or visit iai.tv for more debates and  talks from the world's leading thinkers  on today's biggest ideas  you

